, 0.0125732421875, 0.03118896484375, 0.0211944580078125, -0.0174102783203125, -0.00560760498046875, 0.01119232177734375, 0.0313720703125, -0.00621795654296875, -0.032318115234375, 0.0225372314453125, -0.0008516311645507812, 0.00428009033203125, -0.043243408203125, 0.01120758056640625, 0.008544921875, -0.007297515869140625, -0.019073486328125, -0.037445068359375, 0.033416748046875, -0.03302001953125, 0.0190277099609375, 0.00745391845703125, 0.00482940673828125, -0.039520263671875, -0.0199737548828125, -0.0036754608154296875, -0.0245208740234375, -0.00493621826171875, -0.029815673828125, 0.01092529296875, 0.00801849365234375, 0.02239990234375, -0.0401611328125, -0.0024662017822265625, 0.0090789794921875, -0.00020956993103027344, -0.0018138885498046875, -0.0214691162109375, 0.0102996826171875, -0.00589752197265625, -0.0178985595703125, -0.00399017333984375, 0.0110015869140625, -0.0031280517578125, 0.01873779296875, 0.0323486328125, 0.0294036865234375, 0.00399017333984375, -0.031646728515625, -0.0164031982421875, -0.007843017578125, -0.0048370361328125, 0.00604248046875, 0.020782470703125, -0.01273345947265625, -0.038909912109375, 0.001552581787109375, -0.01202392578125, -0.023223876953125, 0.0204315185546875, -0.0027370452880859375, 0.0034847259521484375, 0.0241546630859375, 0.01082611083984375, -0.005367279052734375, -0.0031604766845703125, -0.0265960693359375, 0.0123138427734375, 0.008880615234375, -0.003200531005859375, 0.011688232421875, 0.0217437744140625, 0.00823974609375, 0.0014972686767578125, 0.021026611328125, 0.0226898193359375, 0.005374908447265625, 0.005565643310546875, -0.041839599609375, 0.07940673828125, -0.0112152099609375, -0.00885772705078125, -0.005077362060546875, -0.017730712890625, -0.0055389404296875, 0.03729248046875, -0.0009870529174804688, 0.0263671875, -0.0060577392578125, 0.0458984375, -0.01372528076171875, 0.027252197265625, 0.00981903076171875, 0.011688232421875, -0.0118560791015625, -0.01401519775390625, -0.02984619140625, 0.01690673828125, -0.035430908203125, -0.005527496337890625, 0.055267333984375, 0.017669677734375, 0.007762908935546875, 0.0094757080078125, 0.0142669677734375, 0.0196533203125, 0.01119232177734375, -0.0088653564453125, -0.02734375, 0.01172637939453125, 0.01399993896484375, 0.01525115966796875, -0.00688934326171875, 0.00888824462890625, -0.038787841796875, -0.014251708984375, 0.0026950836181640625, 0.00882720947265625, 0.005950927734375, 0.00481414794921875, -0.01323699951171875, 0.007350921630859375, -0.013519287109375, -0.0089874267578125, 0.015106201171875, 0.0302886962890625, 0.032562255859375, 0.01812744140625, -0.01052093505859375, 0.00684356689453125, 0.02044677734375, -0.007350921630859375, 0.052459716796875, -0.0230255126953125, 0.007785797119140625, 0.0150604248046875, -0.0355224609375, 0.0243072509765625, 0.0015239715576171875, -0.006317138671875, 0.0033168792724609375, -0.0289459228515625, 0.0011730194091796875, 0.005275726318359375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "District heating networks are a central tool to achieve low-carbon heat supplies. However, they face significant challenges due to increasingly heterogeneous, partially time-varying renewable sources, the integration of thermal storage, and the emergence of meshed (cyclic) topologies\u2014network structures that contain closed loops or cycles. These factors complicate the operation and optimization of such networks, as they introduce variability, operational constraints, and increased computational complexity. Addressing these challenges is crucial for ensuring efficient, reliable, and sustainable heat distribution in modern energy systems.\n\n**Terminology and Definitions:**  \n- *Global optimization* refers to finding the best possible solution (global optimum) to the network operation problem, as opposed to local optima that may not be optimal overall.\n- *Optimality gap* is the difference between the best known solution and the theoretical global optimum, expressed as a percentage of the optimum; a lower gap indicates a solution closer to the true optimum.\n- *Meshed topology* refers to a network structure that contains cycles or closed loops, as opposed to a tree-like (acyclic) structure.\n- *Primal heuristics* are algorithmic strategies that attempt to quickly find feasible solutions by making informed guesses about variable assignments, often used to accelerate optimization algorithms.\n\n**Introduction:**  \nDistrict heating networks distribute heat from multiple suppliers to consumers through interconnected pipelines. The integration of renewable sources and thermal storage increases the variability and unpredictability of supply, while cycles (closed loops) in the network topology\u2014referred to as meshed topologies\u2014complicate flow management and optimization. These challenges can lead to inefficiencies, increased operational costs, and difficulties in ensuring reliable service. Therefore, developing effective optimization methods that can handle these complexities is essential for the successful operation of district heating systems.\n\n**Methodology:**  \nTo address the optimization challenges in district heating networks, we introduce several new methodological ingredients, each targeting a specific aspect of the problem. The main contributions and their implementation details are:\n\n1. **Exclusion of cyclic flow:**  \n   Cyclic flow refers to the unnecessary circulation of heat carrier fluid in closed loops within the network, which can waste energy and reduce efficiency. Our method systematically identifies cycles in the network graph using cycle basis algorithms and enforces flow direction constraints by introducing binary variables that restrict flow directions along cycle edges. This eliminates non-productive circulation, reducing the feasible solution space and improving optimization performance.\n\n2. **Symmetry exploitation between supply and return networks:**  \n   Many district heating networks have similar or mirrored structures for supply and return pipelines. We detect such symmetries by analyzing the network topology and mapping nodes and edges between supply and return sides. The algorithm then solves for one side and applies the solution to the other, avoiding redundant computations and effectively halving the computational effort for symmetric cases.\n\n3. **Reduction of temperature mixing constraints:**  \n   Temperature mixing constraints ensure that the temperature at network junctions remains within operational limits when flows from different sources mix. We analyze the network structure to identify junctions where mixing could potentially violate temperature limits, using graph traversal and flow analysis. Redundant constraints at junctions with single-source inflows or where temperature limits are never active are removed, decreasing the number of constraints the solver must handle and speeding up computation.\n\n4. **Novel primal heuristics and branching rules:**  \n   Primal heuristics are strategies used to quickly find feasible solutions by making educated guesses about variable assignments. We introduce heuristics that prioritize assigning flow directions along major supply routes and at critical junctions, based on demand and supply profiles. Improved branching rules are implemented by ranking variables according to their impact on feasibility and optimality, such as those related to high-capacity pipes or tight temperature constraints. These techniques help the solver converge more rapidly to high-quality solutions.\n\n**Evaluation:**  \nThe proposed methods are evaluated on a set of generated and real-world benchmark network instances with cycles and several suppliers. Evaluation metrics include:\n- **Number of instances solved to global optimality:** Measures the algorithm's ability to find the best possible solution for each instance.\n- **Total runtime:** Reflects computational efficiency.\n- **Solution quality (optimality gap):** Quantifies how close the obtained solution is to the global optimum.\n\nA summary of results is as follows:\n\n| Instance Type      | Baseline: Solved/Total | Baseline: Avg. Runtime (min) | Baseline: Avg. Gap (%) | Proposed: Solved/Total | Proposed: Avg. Runtime (min) | Proposed: Avg. Gap (%) |\n|--------------------|-----------------------|------------------------------|------------------------|------------------------|------------------------------|------------------------|\n| Generated (20)     | 7/20                  | 45                           | 3.2                    | 16/20                  | 18                           | 0.8                    |\n| Real-world (5)     | 2/5                   | >60                          | 2.5                    | 5/5                    | 8\u201355                         | <1.0                   |\n\nOn generated benchmark instances, the new methods more than double the number of solved instances and more than halve the runtime compared to baseline spatial branch-and-bound algorithms. For real-world instances, all cases are solved with an optimality gap below 1% in run times ranging from several minutes to under an hour, depending on network size.\n\n**Time Decomposition and Assumptions:**  \nFor multiple time steps that are coupled by a storage, a time decomposition approach is investigated. This approach assumes that storage operation can be decoupled across time steps when storage levels are fixed at the beginning and end of the horizon, and that demand and supply profiles are known in advance. These assumptions are practically reasonable in many planning and operational contexts where storage schedules and demand forecasts are available. However, if storage levels are not well-defined or if demand and supply are highly uncertain, the approach may yield suboptimal or infeasible solutions. In such cases, the accuracy and applicability of the method may be limited, and additional techniques to handle uncertainty or flexible storage operation would be required. The impact of these assumptions is that, while the method is efficient and yields optimal solutions under fixed and known conditions, its applicability diminishes in highly dynamic or uncertain environments. Under the stated assumptions, the approach is shown to yield an optimal solution, but its use should be carefully considered in cases with significant uncertainty or flexible storage operation.\n\n**Example Application:**  \nOn a small example network consisting of 10 nodes, 3 suppliers, and 2 storage units, the decomposition is able to compute optimal solutions in less than a second, while solving the complete time-coupled problem is not possible within one hour. In this network, suppliers are connected to different nodes, and storage units are placed at strategic locations to buffer supply and demand fluctuations. The results were validated by comparing the decomposed solution to the best-known solution from the full time-coupled model (when available), confirming that the decomposition yields the same optimal objective value under the stated assumptions. While this example demonstrates the practical applicability and efficiency of the proposed methods for small networks, scaling to larger or more complex systems may introduce additional computational challenges. However, the observed improvements in runtime and solution quality on benchmark instances suggest that the methods are promising for larger networks, especially when network symmetry and cycle structure are present.\n\n**Conclusion and Future Research Directions:**  \nThe introduced techniques\u2014cyclic flow exclusion, symmetry exploitation, reduction of temperature mixing constraints, and novel heuristics\u2014significantly improve the tractability of global optimization for district heating networks with complex topologies and time-coupled storage. The greatest improvements are observed in networks with pronounced symmetry and multiple cycles. For operators and policymakers, these advances enable more efficient and reliable planning and operation of district heating systems, supporting the integration of renewable sources and the transition to low-carbon energy. While the methods are effective under the stated assumptions, future research could address several areas: (1) developing robust optimization techniques to handle greater uncertainty in demand and supply, (2) extending the approach to more flexible or real-time storage operation, (3) improving scalability for very large or highly interconnected networks, and (4) integrating additional operational constraints such as maintenance scheduling or dynamic pricing. Overall, these advances make global optimization approaches more suitable for real-world district heating applications."}}